  "style": {
    "id": "layer-print",
    "name": "Layer Print",
    "description": "A generic desktop 3D printer in a bright workshop: an open aluminium frame, a dark gridded heated bed, a gantry that rises with the part and a print head with a glowing brass nozzle laying down coloured filament. The object grows layer by layer and every layer is drawn as its real toolpath — outer perimeter, inner perimeter, then infill (scan lines on the floor, a zigzag inside the wall) — from a slicer plan (slicer.js): the part is sliced once into timed polylines, the whole bead geometry is built once with a per-vertex deposit time, and a frame shows every bead whose time has passed, cut exactly at the nozzle. The planned path of the current layer shows ahead of the nozzle as a thin line, fresh plastic glows for a moment, and the finished part keeps fine layer ridges. Print time is compressed honestly: timing stages speed up layer after layer. Stepper servo/whir cues per move and a tick per layer change come from the same plan.",
